# Accessibility Standards (axe-core / WCAG 2 AA)

The E2E a11y shard runs **axe-core (WCAG 2 A/AA)** against the deployed dev pages. Radix/shadcn
primitives handle most ARIA automatically, but a recurring set of *serious* violations kept
surfacing on custom viz and on primitives used with placeholder-only content. Fix these at author
time on **every** new UI surface (admin dashboards, the authenticated portals, public pages, forms) —
don't wait for the crawl to flag them.

## The latent-a11y trap (why these surfaced late)
axe on a page that renders **blank / errored / empty-state** passes **vacuously** — there are no
elements to violate. In 16.38d, once the API-Gateway fix let authed admin/portal pages finally
render **real data**, the 16.21 crawl exercised surfaces it never had and surfaced **13 serious
violations at once**. Lesson: a "green" a11y run against an unauthenticated/empty page is a **false
pass**. Assert a11y against **fully-rendered, authenticated, data-populated** pages, and treat new
data-bearing components as unaudited until the crawl has actually reached them.

## Recurring violations → fixes (apply proactively)

- **`aria-progressbar-name`** — every element with `role="progressbar"` needs an accessible name.
  Inline "breakdown bar" divs (`aria-valuenow/min/max` but no name) fail. Add an `aria-label`
  describing the datum:
  - ❌ `<div role="progressbar" aria-valuenow={n} aria-valuemin={0} aria-valuemax={max} />`
  - ✅ `<div role="progressbar" aria-label={`${label}: ${value}`} aria-valuenow={n} … />`
  - When a shared bar component has no label in scope, add a required `label` prop and pass it from
    every call site (coerce optional lookups: `label={LABELS[k] ?? k}` to satisfy `string`).

- **`button-name`** — a button with no text needs an accessible name. shadcn `SelectTrigger` whose
  `SelectValue` shows only a **placeholder** renders a nameless button; icon-only buttons too.
  - ✅ `<SelectTrigger aria-label="Document decision">…</SelectTrigger>`; icon buttons get
    `aria-label` (and decorative icons `aria-hidden`).

- **`listitem` / `aria-required-children`** — `<li>` must sit in a `<ul>`/`<ol>` (or `role=list`).
  Putting `role="feed"` on an `<ol>` changes its required children to `role="article"`, so the
  `<li>`s become invalid children and BOTH rules fail. Don't bolt landmark/widget roles onto
  native list elements:
  - ❌ `<ol role="feed"><li>…</li></ol>` → ✅ `<ol><li>…</li></ol>` (drop `role="feed"`).

- **`dlitem`** — `<dt>`/`<dd>` MUST be **direct children of a `<dl>`**. A `<div>` wrapping a
  `<dt>`/`<dd>` pair breaks it:
  - ❌ `<div><dt/><dd/></div>` → ✅ `<dl><dt/><dd/></dl>`.

- **`color-contrast`** (needs ≥ 4.5:1 for normal text, ≥ 3:1 for large text, in **both** themes):
  - Light-mode `text-amber-600` fails on light backgrounds → use `text-amber-700`
    (`text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-300`). The same caution applies to other `-600` accent text.
  - Don't dim an already-muted token with an opacity modifier: `text-muted-foreground/80` drops
    below AA → use full `text-muted-foreground`.
  - Verify contrast in **light AND dark** — a token that passes in one mode can fail in the other.

## Baseline expectations (see also `react-frontend.mdc` → Accessibility)
- All interactive elements keyboard-accessible; prefer Radix/shadcn primitives (they wire ARIA).
- Every form input has a visible label or `aria-label`.
- Custom visualizations built from `div`s (bars, charts, gauges) need explicit names/roles — the
  primitive layer won't add them for you.
